# Home Assistant controls and flows

Use these specifications when you design or review a household task in Home Assistant. Each one gives the states a person can see, how to word them, how to check the task and where the native interface stays in charge. They combine the civica [brand rules](../styles/colour.html#colour-rules), [writing guide](../content/index.html) and reusable components with what each integration really provides.

The catalogue covers the 30 design gaps in the [component audit](../community/missing-components.html), plus further families found by the [item-by-item interface census](interface-coverage.html). Use the census to find a built-in card, selector, control, settings route or domain. Every example is simulated. It does not connect to a household, replace a native editor or show that an installed integration supports a command.

The [A to Z Technical lexicon](../content/style-guides/technical-a-to-z.html) gives native names and literal keys. The [UK design systems review](../community/uk-design-system-review.html) records the public-service pattern each specification draws on, or why civica extends it.

## Start with state and actions

Read [entity state, confidence and freshness](entity-state.html) before you format a reading. Read [action results](action-results.html) before you add a command. Together they keep a request, its acceptance and the state the device later reports apart. They also keep an unavailable entity, an unknown value, a real zero and an old retained value apart.

